  "comments": "Two main groups of Reverse are known.<br /><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">Reverse</span><br />\r\nDie <span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">A</span> - first numeral in date &#039;1&#039; with short nose.<br />\r\nDie <span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">B</span> - first numeral in date &#039;1&#039; with long nose.<br />\r\n<a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5f74f1966fed7.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5f74f1966fed7.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"192\" height=\"250\" /></a><br /><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">Combinations</span><br />\r\nDie <span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">A</span> 1948-1974, 1976-1981.<br />\r\nDie <span style=\"font-weight:bold;\">B</span> 1975.<br /><br />\r\n<span style=\"font-style:italic;\">Note. </span>In 1961, 1971 and 1981 the style of second numeral &#039;1&#039; in date is with long nose, being the first with short one. It is not clear, why the appearance of numeral &#039;1&#039; was not consistent during the emission and the change of &#039;1&#039; happened just for one year in 1975.<br /><br />\r\nKM# 135.1 = Coin alignment<br />\r\nKM# 135.2 = Medal alignment",
